A||N||7||HummelHund||Shifting Problems||||5||
Z||000000||HummelHund||04-02-2001||09:40 PM||||I'm close to buying an '84 SE, white w/ gray int. 4-cyl, manual trans. It has some shifting problems, though, and I need some help to see if they're major or minor problems. Here's the rundown. When you try to shift through the gears, they are hard to find, but are there if you work at it. Once in gear the car moves forward and backward (we only tried 1st and reverse) easily, with no slippage (at least for the few feet we went). What does this mean? I've had responses from the Fiero mailing lists saying it's the hydraulic clutch fluid that needs bleeding and replacement, that it's worn out or out of adjustment linkage, a bent pedal, or a major transmission problem. I can deal with clutch fluid or linkage, but I need to know if it sounds like it could be a major tranny problem, because I can't deal with that. Do the symptoms the car's having suggest a bad tranny or one of the easier fixes? Other than the shifting problem, the other problems the car has seem minor, it surges at idle (diagnosed as a sensor of some sort), it needs two new tires, the headlights don't go up or down(cranking is fine for me), and the alternator belt is doing the fiero loosening thing and squealing like a banshee. I just need to know as many opinions as possible about the shifting problem before I make my decision.<BR>Thanks, <BR>Sean Wilson||||reg||5
Z||000001||HummelHund||04-02-2001||09:53 PM|||| In addition to the original message, just to clarify things, the car shifted badly while turned off, I haven't checked it while moving yet. Does thsi help?<BR>Thanks,<BR>Sean Wilson||||reg||1
Z||000002||Taijiguy||04-02-2001||09:54 PM||||The cable setup on these isn't the most accurate shifting method made, and take a bit of getting used to, but if it's excessive trying to find the gears, maybe the cables need adjusted. <BR>With the engine running, if it grinds going into reverse, or is hard to get into first, it's possible the clutch hydraulics might need bleeding, worst case is it needs a new clutch. You'll only know that after troubleshooting the hydraulics.<BR>Good luck!<BR>~Taiji||||reg||1
Z||000003||Wade87GT||04-02-2001||10:00 PM||||Hey Neighbor,<P>I just went through the entire clutch thing on my 87 GT and might have some tips for you.<P>Wade (from the next town over)||||reg||1
Z||000004||RacinRob||04-02-2001||10:26 PM||||to go on what Taijiguy it took me a week before I could shift my Fiero right, and even after I drove it for a few month I still miss third once in a while. I have an '85 4 speed. ||||reg||1
Z||000005||HummelHund||04-02-2001||10:35 PM||||I have shifted my friend's '84 4-speed and even thuogh it did shift horribly compared to other stick shifts I've driven, I could still get it into every gear, reverse was the only one I had trouble with. The '84 SE in question shifts extremely badly, worse than any normal 4-speed '84. I'll go out and drive the car some (the gears are there, you just have to hunt a little) and report back to you about how it shifts under power, etc.<BR>Sean W.||||reg||1
Z||000006||Carrolles||04-03-2001||08:14 AM||||I'd try adjusting the shift cables first.  The Haynes manual should give the procedure.||||reg||1
Z||000007||HummelHund||04-03-2001||10:16 AM||||I've made a sort of checklist to go through when I get there, I hope it sounds good.<BR>1. Check if the car shifts good without depressing the clutch pedal and engaging the clutch. If it does, it's a clutch problem of some sort, check for a bent clutch pedal. If no bent clutch pedal, it's the clutch, run screaming from the car.<BR>1a. Also check for pedal pressure. If it's too easy to depress, pump and see if pressure builds. if it does, try bleeding the clutch. If that doesn't help, inspect the master and slave cylinders.<BR>2. If it shifts badly without the clutch engaged, go to the engine compartment and shift from the transmission. If it readily goes into each gear from there, it's something cable related.<BR>3. If it does not, check the gear oil level. If it's low, refill and try shifting again. If that doesn't help, run screaming from the car.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><BR>Is this a fairly complete checklist? If it isn't please post revisions or corrections, it'd help me a lot.<BR>Sean W.||||reg||5
